WEBVTT

00:00:12.762 --> 00:00:17.475
( quiet, mysterious melody
plays )

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:28.194
♪ ♪

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:38.580
( tempo increases )

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.299
♪ ♪

00:00:57.515 --> 00:01:00.602
( pounding percussion
joins in )

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:07.317
Captioning sponsored by
WARNER BROS.

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:09.319
♪ ♪

00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:30.423
♪ ♪

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.528
♪ ♪

00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:57.450
( whirring )

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:18.388
♪ ♪

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:39.200
♪ ♪

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:43.163
( quiet mysterious melody
returns )

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:51.921
( tempo becomes quietly
frenetic )

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.193
♪ ♪

00:03:35.089 --> 00:03:38.259
( tempo crescendos and quiets )

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.438
♪ ♪

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:10.500
♪ ♪

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:14.003
( music fades )

00:04:14.045 --> 00:04:16.005
NARRATOR:
This is a story

00:04:16.047 --> 00:04:20.343
of an ordinary little boy
named Charlie Bucket.

00:04:20.385 --> 00:04:23.930
He was not faster or stronger

00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:26.349
or more clever
than other children.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:32.647
His family was not rich
or powerful or well-connected.

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:36.067
In fact, they barely
had enough to eat.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:41.281
Charlie Bucket was the luckiest
boy in the entire world.

00:04:41.364 --> 00:04:44.033
He just didn’t know it yet.

00:04:48.871 --> 00:04:50.957
♪ ♪

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:02.844
Evening, Buckets.

00:05:02.885 --> 00:05:04.721
Evening.
Hi, Dad.

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.434
Soup’s almost ready, darling.

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:11.060
Uh, don’t suppose
there’s anything

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.437
extra to put in?

00:05:14.063 --> 00:05:15.356
Oh, well.

00:05:15.398 --> 00:05:17.525
Nothing goes better with cabbage
than cabbage.

00:05:20.862 --> 00:05:21.946
Charlie...

00:05:23.072 --> 00:05:25.366
I found something
I think you’ll like.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:33.499
NARRATOR:
Charlie’s father worked at
the local toothpaste factory.

00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:37.628
The hours were long
and the pay was terrible.

00:05:37.712 --> 00:05:42.925
Yet, occasionally, there
were unexpected surprises.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.929
It’s exactly what I need.

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:49.599
What is it, Charlie?

00:05:55.271 --> 00:05:57.106
Dad found it--

00:05:57.148 --> 00:05:58.399
just the piece
I needed.

00:05:58.441 --> 00:05:59.317
What piece was it?

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.235
A head for Willy Wonka.

00:06:01.277 --> 00:06:02.528
How wonderful.

00:06:02.612 --> 00:06:04.572
It’s quite a likeness.

00:06:04.614 --> 00:06:05.907
CHARLIE:
You think so?

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:08.951
Think so?
I know so.

00:06:08.993 --> 00:06:12.455
I saw Willy Wonka
with my own two eyes.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:14.082
I used to work for him,
you know.

00:06:14.123 --> 00:06:15.792
You did?
I did.

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:16.376
He did.

00:06:16.459 --> 00:06:17.377
He did.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.921
I love grapes.

00:06:19.962 --> 00:06:24.550
Of course, I was a much
younger man in those days.

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:27.053
Willy Wonka began

00:06:27.136 --> 00:06:30.640
with a single store
on Cherry Street.

00:06:30.681 --> 00:06:33.434
But the whole world
wanted his candy.

00:06:37.188 --> 00:06:38.189
Mr. Wonka.

00:06:38.272 --> 00:06:39.232
Yeah.

00:06:39.315 --> 00:06:40.566
We need more

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.653
Wonka bars and we’re
out of chocolate birds.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:45.405
Birds?

00:06:45.488 --> 00:06:47.156
Birds.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:48.491
Well, then,

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:50.243
we’ll need to make some more.

00:06:50.326 --> 00:06:51.828
Here.

00:06:53.996 --> 00:06:55.331
Now open.

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:58.376
( cheeping )

00:06:58.459 --> 00:06:59.627
( giggles )

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:02.213
The man was a genius.

00:07:02.296 --> 00:07:04.090
Did you know he invented

00:07:04.173 --> 00:07:07.927
a new way of making chocolate
ice cream, so that it stays

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:11.055
cold for hours
without a freezer?

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:14.183
You can even leave it lying
in the sun on a hot day

00:07:14.225 --> 00:07:15.476
and it won’t go runny.

00:07:15.518 --> 00:07:17.353
That’s impossible.

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:19.814
But Willy Wonka did it.

00:07:22.233 --> 00:07:23.484
Before long, he decided

00:07:23.526 --> 00:07:27.155
to build a proper
chocolate factory--

00:07:27.196 --> 00:07:30.825
the largest chocolate factory
in history--

00:07:30.867 --> 00:07:33.661
50 times as big as any other.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:41.043
( cheering )

